## Synchronization, failure and growth

Ordinary Studio transitions already refresh the gallery and activity display.
They also refresh the automatic Flow and Axis page, including after image adoption.
State artifact writers notify this projection when they write a known source or
referenced scene context. A changed scene, new scene file, deleted file, narrative
revision or changed accepted image changes the projection identity.
Files edited in an external editor are not watched by a service:
run `studio.py sync` after saving. This rechecks the base, events, processes,
configuration, narrative/scene files in both conventional locations, collection
membership and explicit artwork/evidence dependencies. The alternative location
is part of that check even when it has no authored sources, so a newly appearing
series cannot be silently ignored during publication. Already open pages show
their printed input revision until reloaded; reloading the page refreshes
the view, not the source files or the story itself.

The output contains its observed inputs, implementation identity and generated time.
An input records present bytes with their actual hash, absence, or an access
failure without a content hash. Optional input reads allow absence; they still
report access failures. Each path is observed once under each read contract during model
construction. A sheet-relative read keeps its boundary in that contract, so an
earlier read under another boundary cannot bypass the sheet's checks.

A current default page's publication receipt keeps its failed source paths known
to writer notifications. A fatal failure does not retain that input list, and a
custom view alone does not supply the default notification route. Recover the
default page with `studio.py sync`; rerun the selected `story_timeline.py --out`
command to refresh a custom view.

Inputs are freshly rechecked before and after rendering, before atomic HTML
publication. Present inputs must still have the same bytes; failed access must
still produce the same refusal. The latter makes no claim about unknown content
or the identity of a refused filesystem entry. A repaired, removed or newly
unreadable source changes its observation and prevents publication of the old
model. Repeating an unchanged sync keeps the same page and does not duplicate
its activity event.

An unchanged page is reused before compiling the ledger or resolving its views.
This reuse checks the exact HTML and embedded model against the publication
receipt, the selected configuration and viewpoint, current installed builder
sources and schemas, every observed input's actual bytes or refusal, and scene
collection membership. It does not substitute timestamps, file sizes or an
event sequence for content equality. Added and removed sources and changes in
the alternative story location therefore invalidate reuse, including changes
made outside the Studio commands. The installed source check covers dynamically
selected schemas and renderer templates; it does not scan the user's packs or
other workspaces.

The view schema and HTML templates are read from that acquired implementation.
Publication rechecks the installed source identity alongside the Studio inputs,
so an implementation edit during rendering cannot publish a page with an
earlier fingerprint describing later template bytes.

Missing or damaged derived publication data is rebuilt from those sources.
An unrelated file at an output or receipt path remains protected. Reuse is
local to this display: a cached page supplies no event-validation result,
adoption approval or permission to a generation or canonical change. Those
operations acquire and verify their own current evidence.
Projection actions enter the operator activity journal, never `state/events.jsonl`.

An unreadable scene, referenced context, local evidence or declared artwork can
remain a source notice on a current page when its observed failure is unchanged.
Malformed JSON or other invalid content still carries the hash of the bytes
that were read. No unavailable source supplies a guessed link or world state.
If required configuration or ledger inputs cannot be loaded, series selection
fails, an input changes during publication, or rendering fails, the current page
becomes an explicit unavailable view. The prior successful view is retained as `timeline.last-good.html`
with a visible STALE banner. An inaccessible output path may prevent even that
warning from being written; the operation diagnostic and pending-projection marker
then require `studio.py sync`. A story-view failure does not undo an image adoption
or prevent an independent gallery/activity refresh.

One compiled ledger validates artifact structure once and indexes IDs, timelines
and revisions. Flow reads each scene source once and does not request a new replay per
card. Scene IDs, participants, associations and relation directions are indexed.
A corrupt draft remains visible as a source notice; it supplies no guessed scene link.
Duplicate scene IDs are shown without choosing the newest file as canonical.

The browser filters recorded data and creates a bounded page: 24 Flow records,
40 Axis records and 40 state fields. Full source data remains embedded in the HTML;
paging limits DOM work, not total file size or parsing memory. A single very large
scene can still be expensive to inspect. No unbounded scalability is claimed.
Image verification follows explicit bindings, not a recursive scan of all outputs.
Replay work grows with selected/checkpoint queries and their applicable history;
large diagnostic histories can still take time. No history is truncated or silently
removed to make a view appear successful.

